00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.200
One of the most exciting developments with sequence to sequence models has been the rise of very accurate speech recognition.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:19.000
We're nearing the end of the course, but I want to take just a couple of videos to give you a sense of how these sequence to sequence models are applied to audio data, such as the speech.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.760
So what is the speech recognition problem?

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:29.760
You're given an audio clip X and your job is to automatically find a text transcript Y.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:36.760
So an audio clip, if you plot it, looks like this. The horizontal axis here is time.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.760
And what a microphone does is it really measures minuscule changes in air pressure.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:50.960
And the way you're hearing my voice right now is that your ear is detecting little changes in air pressure, probably generated.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:01:00.960
Either by your speakers or by a headset. And so an audio clip like this plots, you know, basically air pressure against time.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:13.960
And if this audio clip is of me saying the quick brown fox, then hopefully a speech recognition algorithm can input that audio clip and output that transcript.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:24.960
And because even the human ear doesn't process raw waveforms, but the human ear has physical structures that measures the amounts of intensity of different frequencies.

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:34.560
There is a common pre-processing step for audio data is to run your audio clip and generate a spectrogram.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:46.560
So this is a plot where the horizontal axis is time and the vertical axis is frequencies and intensity of different colors shows the amount of energy.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:51.160
So how loud is the sound at different frequencies at different times?

00:01:51.360 --> 00:02:06.160
And so these types of spectrograms, or you might also hear people talk about filter bank outputs, is often commonly applied pre-processing step before audio is passed into a learning algorithm.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:12.560
And the human ear does a computation pretty similar to this pre-processing step.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:24.360
So one of the most exciting trends in speech recognition is that once upon a time, speech recognition systems used to be built using phonemes.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.960
And these were, I want to say, hand engineered basic units of sound.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:33.960
So the quick brown fox represented as phonemes.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:42.160
I'm going to simplify a bit, but you say, the has a duh and an eh sound and quick has a kuh and wuh, eek, kuh sound.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:49.960
And linguists used to write out these basic units of sound and try to break language down into these basic units of sound.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.160
So brown, right?

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:56.560
These aren't the official phonemes which are written with more complicated notation.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:07.960
But linguists used to hypothesize that writing down audio in terms of these basic units of sound called phonemes would be the best way to do speech recognition.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:14.960
But with end-to-end deep learning, we're finding that phoneme representations are no longer necessary.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:25.960
But instead, you can build systems that input an audio clip and directly output a transcript without needing to use hand engineered representations

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:33.160
like these. One of the things that made this possible was going to much larger data sets.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:48.960
So academic data sets on speech recognition might be as 300 hours and in academia, a 3000 hour data set of transcribed audio would be considered reasonable size.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:55.760
So a lot of research has been done. A lot of research papers have been written on data sets that are several thousand hours.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:04.760
But the best commercial systems are now trained on over 10,000 hours and sometimes over 100,000 hours of audio.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:18.760
And it's really moving to much larger audio data sets, transcribed audio data sets with both X and Y, together with deep learning algorithms that has driven a lot of progress in speech recognition.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:25.560
So how do you build a speech recognition system? In the last video, we'll talk about the attention model.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:42.560
So one thing you could do is actually do that where on the horizontal axis, you take in different time frames of the audio input and then you have an attention model, try to output the transcript like the quick brown fox or what was said.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:57.360
One of the methods that seems to work well is to use the CTC costs for speech recognition. CTC stands for connectionless temporal classification and is due to Alex Gray, Santiago Fernandez, Faustina Gomez and Jeroen Schmitt-Huber.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:11.360
So here's the idea. Let's say the audio clip was of someone saying the quick brown fox. We're going to use a neural network structured like this with an equal number of input X's and output Y's.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:23.160
And I've drawn a simple of one unidirectional for only RNN for this, but in practice, this will usually be a bidirectional LSTM or bidirectional GIU and usually a deeper model.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:35.160
But notice that the number of time steps here is very large and in speech recognition, usually the number of input time steps is much bigger than the number of output time steps.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:47.960
So for example, if you have 10 seconds of audio and your features come at 100 Hz, so 100 samples per second, then a 10 second audio clip would end up with a thousand inputs.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:57.960
So it's 100 Hz times 10 seconds ends up with a thousand inputs. But your output might not have a thousand alphabets, might not have a thousand characters.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:12.760
So what do you do? The CTC cost function allows the RNN to generate an output like this. TTT, there's a special character called a blank character, which I'm going to write as an underscore here.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:31.560
H blank, E E E blank blank blank. And then maybe a space, I'm going to write this so that's a space and then blank blank blank, Q Q Q blank blank.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:49.770
And this is considered a correct output for the first part of the space quick with the Q and the basic rule for the CTC cost function is to collapse repeated characters not separated by blank.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:07:06.570
So to be clear, I'm using this underscore to denote the special blank character and that's different than the space character. So there is a space here between the and quick so I should output a space.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:21.570
But by collapsing repeated characters not separated by blank, it actually collapse the sequence into T H E and then space and Q.

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:38.370
And this allows the network to have a thousand outputs by repeating characters a lot of times or inserting a bunch of blank characters and still end up with a much shorter output text transfer.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:08:00.170
So this phrase here, the quick brown fox, including spaces, actually has nineteen characters. And if somehow the neural network is forced output a thousand characters by allowing the network to insert blanks and repeated characters, you can still represent this nineteen character output with this one thousand outputs values of Y.

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:13.170
So this paper by Alex Grace as well as by do's deep speech, speech recognition system, which I was involved in, use this idea to build effective speech recognition systems.

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:26.170
So I hope that gives you a rough sense of how speech recognition models work. Attention like models work and CTC models work and present two different options of how to go about building these systems.

00:08:26.170 --> 00:08:36.170
Now today, building a effective production scale speech recognition system is a pretty significant effort and requires a very large dataset.

